:RE_Ballo_LeaveNaples a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Verdi rejected the most drastic demanded alterations, broke the contract with Teatro San Carlo, and moved the project to Rome." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Giger1999_Ballo,
        :SRC_Ricordi_BalloCritical ;
    :eventDate 1858 ;
    :hasOutcome :Abandonment ;
    :performedBy :GiuseppeVerdi ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Ballo_Regicide ;
    :usesStrategy :InstitutionalCircumvention .

:RE_Ballo_Transposition a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Verdi and Somma transformed the setting and identities while preserving the dramatic structure: Sweden became colonial Boston, Gustav III became Governor Riccardo, and other characters were renamed." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Giger1999_Ballo,
        :SRC_Met_Ballo,
        :SRC_Ricordi_BalloCritical ;
    :eventDate "1858-1859" ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :AntonioSomma,
        :GiuseppeVerdi ;
    :producesVersion :UnBalloInMaschera1859 ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Ballo_SovereignRepresentation ;
    :usesStrategy :CharacterTransformation,
        :HistoricalDisplacement,
        :NarrativeTransposition .

:RE_Bulgakov_AesopianExpression a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "The novel uses satire, ambiguity, fantastic displacement, and passages that scholars have discussed in terms of Aesopian language: indirect expression capable of carrying concealed political or social meanings." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Pope1977_Bulgakov,
        :SRC_Rosenshield1997_Bulgakov ;
    :eventDate "1930s" ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :MikhailBulgakov ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Bulgakov_PoliticalExpression ;
    :resultsInWork :MasterAndMargarita ;
    :usesStrategy :AesopianLanguage,
        :Ambiguity,
        :IndirectRepresentation,
        :Satire .

:RE_HKR_IronicNarration a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Braun frames the critique in an ironic, Diderot-like master-servant narrative with an intrusive narrator who comments on what 'cannot be said directly'." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Darnton1991,
        :SRC_Lance_Metailie ;
    :eventDate 1981 ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :VolkerBraun ;
    :respondsTo :CON_HKR_Ideology ;
    :usesStrategy :Allegory,
        :Irony .

:RE_HKR_Lesehilfe a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Dieter Schlenstedt, one of the manuscript's first reviewers, writes an appended 'schöngeistige Lesehilfe': five fictional letters that ironise the review process and guide readers; the text was meant both to placate the censors and to guide reading." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_GuibertYeche,
        :SRC_Hoepcke1985,
        :SRC_Lance_Metailie,
        :SRC_bpb_Selbstzensur ;
    :eventDate 1985 ;
    :hasOutcome :AlternativeVersionProduced ;
    :performedBy :DieterSchlenstedt ;
    :producesVersion :HKR_GDR_1985 ;
    :respondsTo :CON_HKR_Ideology ;
    :usesStrategy :Irony,
        :ParatextualFraming .

:RE_MasterMargarita_TextRestoration a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Elena Bulgakova preserved and prepared the text for publication; omitted passages circulated outside the censored journal version, and complete versions were subsequently published abroad, including a complete Russian text." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Curtis2019_Bulgakov,
        :SRC_Pope1977_Bulgakov ;
    :eventDate "1966-1969" ;
    :hasOutcome :AlternativeVersionProduced ;
    :performedBy :ElenaBulgakova ;
    :producesVersion :MasterMargarita_FullRussian1969 ;
    :respondsTo :CON_MasterMargarita_PublicationCuts ;
    :usesStrategy :AlternativePublication,
        :TextualRestoration .

:RE_Nolde_UnpaintedPictures a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Nolde continued working privately and produced further small-format watercolours in Seebüll. Museum accounts describe these works as being made secretly during the period of prohibition." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_NGI_NoldeDegenerate,
        :SRC_NoldeMuseum_Artist ;
    :eventDate "1941-1945" ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :EmilNolde ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Nolde_ProfessionalPractice ;
    :resultsInWork :Nolde_UnpaintedPictures ;
    :usesStrategy :MediumAdaptation,
        :SecretProduction .

:RE_Notorious_FragmentedKiss a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Hitchcock stages a scene of about two and a half to three minutes as a chain of short kisses (none longer than ~3 seconds), interrupted by talk and a telephone call, so the scene stays within the rule but reads as one continuous embrace." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Billheimer2019,
        :SRC_HitchZone_PCA ;
    :eventDate 1946 ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :AlfredHitchcock ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Notorious_Kissing ;
    :usesStrategy :Fragmentation,
        :IndirectRepresentation .

:RE_Panahi_ThisIsNotAFilm a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Panahi and Mojtaba Mirtahmasb made a self-reflexive video diary largely inside Panahi's apartment. Panahi stages and describes an unproduced screenplay and turns the conditions of being forbidden to make films into part of the film's subject and form." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_Cannes_Panahi2011,
        :SRC_Cannes_ThisIsNotAFilm2011,
        :SRC_CinemaIranica_TINAF,
        :SRC_IranHumanRights_Panahi2010 ;
    :eventDate 2011 ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :JafarPanahi,
        :MojtabaMirtahmasb ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Panahi_Filmmaking ;
    :resultsInWork :ThisIsNotAFilm ;
    :usesStrategy :Circumvention,
        :MinimalProduction,
        :SelfReflexivity .

:RE_Shost_FifthSymphony a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Fifth Symphony (composed 18 April–20 July 1937; premiered 21 November 1937, Leningrad Philharmonic, Mravinsky): a turn 'towards greater accessibility, towards greater simplicity', classical form and tonal harmony, with a finale whose triumph has been heard as sincere or ironic." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_FrolovaWalker2023,
        :SRC_Robinson_BSO,
        :SRC_Wiki_Muddle ;
    :eventDate 1937 ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :DmitriShostakovich ;
    :respondsTo :CON_Shost_Formalism ;
    :resultsInWork :ShostakovichSymphony5 ;
    :usesStrategy :Ambiguity .

:RE_Streetcar_ScreenplayRewrite a :CreativeResponse,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    :dataProvenance "documented case" ;
    :description "Kazan and Williams negotiated and altered the screenplay. Williams rewrote the ending so that Stella appears, somewhat ambiguously, to leave Stanley rather than return to him; other restricted material was handled more indirectly." ;
    :documentedBy :SRC_AFI_Streetcar1951,
        :SRC_Szabo2019_Streetcar ;
    :eventDate 1950 ;
    :hasOutcome :CreativeAdaptation ;
    :performedBy :EliaKazan,
